Unexpected replacement after refactor|A plan wants to destroy and recreate an unchanged production resource after its module address changed.|terraform state list|Compare old and new addresses and confirm the remote identity is unchanged. Inspect whether the plan reflects address movement or an actually immutable attribute change.|Add an appropriate moved block or perform a controlled supported state migration. Do not apply a destructive plan merely to align names.|Generate a new full plan and confirm the resource is moved without unwanted replacement and dependent outputs remain correct.
State lock conflict|A deployment fails because the state is locked.|terraform workspace show|Read lock metadata, backend and workspace; identify the owning job and whether it is still active. Check for a concurrent writer before intervening.|Wait for or coordinate with the live operation. Use force-unlock only for a verified stale lock after preserving evidence and confirming no writer remains.|Run a fresh plan with normal locking and verify a single pipeline owns the apply.
Partial apply failure|An apply created some resources before failing on a quota or permission error.|terraform state list|Compare recorded successful resources with remote reality and the error. Do not assume the operation rolled back as a transaction.|Fix the evidenced quota, permission or configuration issue, then create a new full plan. Import or reconcile exceptional unrecorded objects through a controlled process.|Review the new plan for duplicates or deletes, apply safely and verify a subsequent plan has no unexpected changes.
Unplanned console drift|A plan would undo a manual production mitigation made during an incident.|terraform plan|Compare the proposed diff with the incident change record and actual service behavior.|Decide whether to adopt the mitigation into configuration or intentionally revert it after stabilization. Do not blindly apply the old desired state.|Review the resulting plan with the chosen policy and verify the service stays healthy after reconciliation.
Sensitive plan artifact exposed|A CI artifact contains credentials despite variables being marked sensitive.|terraform show tfplan|Determine which values entered saved plans, state or logs and who could access them. Human-output redaction does not remove stored values.|Restrict or remove exposed artifacts under the retention process, rotate compromised credentials and correct artifact access and logging. Keep necessary state recovery controls.|Verify new plans remain protected and new credentials are used successfully without appearing in logs or broadly accessible artifacts.
Count removal replaces wrong instance|Removing one middle list item causes multiple resource changes.|terraform plan|Compare numeric instance addresses with list positions. A shifted index can associate a remaining name with a different existing object.|Move to stable keyed identities with an explicit migration plan, or otherwise preserve intended address mappings. Avoid applying unintended replacements.|Check a full plan for only the intended removal and verify surviving remote identities remain unchanged.
Imported object immediately changes|After import, Terraform proposes a disruptive update to an existing resource.|terraform state show 'RESOURCE_ADDRESS'|Compare imported attributes, configuration defaults and immutable fields. Import establishes identity but does not make configuration match reality.|Write accurate configuration and intentionally decide which differences to manage. Review provider-specific replacement behavior before any apply.|Obtain an expected plan and test dependent behavior after any approved changes.
Wrong environment selected|A plan shows resources from a different environment than expected.|terraform workspace show|Check workspace, backend key, account credentials, region and variable files. A correct workspace name alone does not prove correct credentials.|Stop before apply and correct the target configuration. Regenerate the plan; do not reuse a file created against the wrong target.|Confirm caller identity, backend and expected resource addresses, then review the newly generated plan.
Provider upgrade creates broad diff|Updating provider dependencies produces many unexpected changes.|terraform providers|Compare lock-file versions, release notes and schema/default changes. Separate actual infrastructure drift from changed provider interpretation.|Pin back to the known-compatible version if needed and test the upgrade independently. Resolve migration requirements before combining it with other changes.|Run representative plans and a controlled canary apply; confirm no unrelated replacements before wider rollout.
Targeted recovery leaves drift|A targeted apply fixed one resource, but the system remains inconsistent.|terraform plan|Run a full plan and inspect resources omitted by targeting, including downstream dependencies and outputs.|Reconcile remaining changes through a reviewed full operation. Keep targeting exceptional rather than making it the normal deployment path.|Confirm a subsequent full plan is clean or contains only explicitly accepted differences and application checks pass.
